# --- API ---
DATABASE_URL=postgres://ct:ct@localhost:5433/calorytracker
PORT=4000

# --- AI provider ---
# Which service runs the journal. `anthropic` (default), `anthropic-api` or
# `openai`. All three share the same tool handlers, so a meal is logged
# identically whichever one ran the turn.
AI_PROVIDER=anthropic

# The two Claude providers are the same models reached two different ways, and
# both are permanent — pick by where it runs, not by quality.

# anthropic: runs on your Claude Code subscription — the Agent SDK reads the
# OAuth credentials `claude` writes to ~/.claude/.credentials.json, so there is
# nothing to set here. Right for a personal install: no key, no per-token bill,
# and one person on one box is the shape the Agent SDK is good at. It spawns the
# `claude` binary once per turn, so a turn holds a process for its whole twenty
# seconds — which is why it is not what you want serving other people.

# anthropic-api: the same Claude models over the Messages API, on a metered key.
# No subprocess and no session file, which is what lets the API run more than one
# replica. This is what a deployment serving other people should run.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...   # https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys
#
# Note that setting ANTHROPIC_API_KEY does NOT by itself switch providers — it is
# also what the Agent SDK picks up in preference to the subscription. Set
# AI_PROVIDER=anthropic-api to choose this one.
#
# The input-tokens-per-minute ceiling your key is entitled to, from
# https://console.anthropic.com/settings/limits. Set it on the same day you set
# the key above: it is what governs this lane in tokens rather than in requests,
# and CHAT_LIMIT cannot do that job — it counts requests, and a photo turn and a
# text turn are an order of magnitude apart in tokens.
#
# A bare number applies to every model. Each still gets its own bucket, because
# the limits themselves are per model — name them individually once your tier
# lists different numbers for Haiku and Opus:
#
#   ANTHROPIC_ITPM=2000000
#   ANTHROPIC_ITPM=claude-haiku-4-5:2000000,claude-opus-5:400000,500000
#
# Write down the published limit, not a reduced one: the bucket keeps 20% back
# on its own. It spends against the tokens that actually count toward the
# ceiling — uncached input plus cache writes — so cache reads are correctly
# free here, exactly as they are to the rate limiter upstream.
#
# Unset means no bucket, which is right for a personal install: one person
# cannot outrun a per-minute token limit, and a ceiling nobody chose is worse
# than none. It does nothing on the other two providers — the subscription lane
# shares a budget with your terminal, and OpenAI is a different vendor.
# ANTHROPIC_ITPM=2000000

# How long a prompt-cache entry is kept alive: `1h` (the default) or `5m`.
#
# A write costs 1.25x the input rate at five minutes and 2x at an hour, and the
# cached prefix here — tool definitions plus the static system prompt, about 18k
# tokens — is byte-identical for every account on the deployment. So the hour is
# worth it exactly when it converts turns from cold to warm, and it wins whenever
# it cuts the cold share to less than about 0.64 of what five minutes leaves.
#
# That bar is cleared earlier than it sounds, because turns cluster. Measured on
# 59 production turns across four accounts: 41% arrived within five minutes of
# another, 32% within the hour, 27% cold either way — a median gap of seventeen
# minutes against a mean of eighty-three. Reasoning from the mean is what made an
# earlier version of this file recommend `5m` at small scale; it was wrong by
# about 20% of the per-turn bill.
#
# `5m` is still right for a genuinely idle install — one turn a day, nothing to
# keep warm — where the difference is pennies in either direction.
#
# ---- The deployment moved to 5m on 2026-08-24 -------------------------------
#
# The gap distribution above predicted a 27% cold share. The cost ledger says
# **100% of production turns wrote cache** — every one, over the whole sample.
# Both are true and they are not measuring the same thing: a gap is when the
# next turn arrives, and a write is what the API charged for. A prefix only
# stays resident while *somebody* is running turns, and across five accounts
# nobody is, so the entry is gone before the next turn arrives however short the
# gap looks on a chart of one account.
#
# So the hour was buying residency that nothing came back for, at 2x the write
# rate against 1.25x. Measured on the heaviest account's turns: $0.0942 -> $0.0667,
# a 29% cut on every metered turn. The tier ceilings in `services/plans.ts` are
# priced against the result, so putting this back to `1h` without repricing
# takes the top tier's annual margin from 20% to about 4%.
#
# The hour becomes right again the moment there is enough traffic for turns to
# read what the previous one wrote. Revisit it with volume rather than treating
# either value as settled — and recompute the tables in `plans.ts` when it moves.
ANTHROPIC_CACHE_TTL=5m

# Whose turns run on the Claude Code subscription instead of the metered key,
# comma-separated. Empty — the default — means everybody's turn is billed.
#
# This is the two-lane split made per user rather than per deployment. The
# subscription is a personal login: right for the handful of accounts belonging
# to whoever runs the box, wrong for a stranger who signed up this morning,
# whose turn should be billed and counted like the product it is part of.
#
# It can only ever move somebody *onto* the subscription, never off it, so on a
# personal install already running AI_PROVIDER=anthropic it does nothing at all.
#
# It also lifts the plan meters for those addresses — unlimited messages, photos,
# fridge scans, recipes and meal plans, whatever `users.plan` says. Every ceiling
# in `plans.ts` is priced in dollars, and none are spent here. The burst guard on
# the journal stays: a stuck client would spend your own Claude rate limit, which
# no bill ever tells you about. See SUBSCRIPTIONS.md, "Who the meters do not
# apply to".
# Requires a signed-in `claude` (a ~/.claude/.credentials.json the API can read).
# Without one the addresses here fall through to whatever AI_PROVIDER says rather
# than failing: the Agent SDK would otherwise pick up ANTHROPIC_API_KEY and give
# them the metered lane plus a subprocess, which is the same bill and slower.
# SUBSCRIPTION_EMAILS=me@example.com,partner@example.com

# openai: a metered API key. A ChatGPT subscription does NOT cover API usage —
# these are separate products. Create a key at
# https://platform.openai.com/api-keys
# OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
#
# Whatever model your account can see. Names churn, so this is yours to set.
# OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4o
#
# Optional per-turn overrides, each falling back to OPENAI_MODEL. Worth setting
# when your vendor's line-up is uneven — logging is high volume and wants the
# cheap model, photos need one that can see, the weekly review runs once.
# OPENAI_MODEL_TEXT=
# OPENAI_MODEL_VISION=
# OPENAI_MODEL_SETUP=
# OPENAI_MODEL_REVIEW=
#
# Any OpenAI-compatible endpoint works — Groq, Together, OpenRouter, a local
# Ollama. Point this at its base URL and set OPENAI_API_KEY to that service's key.
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
#
# What a token costs, in USD per million, so the admin panel's cost report can
# price this provider — the API returns tokens but never a price, and the right
# rate depends on which of the above you pointed it at. Leave them unset and the
# panel records tokens and reports the cost as unknown rather than as zero.
# OPENAI_PRICE_INPUT=2.50
# OPENAI_PRICE_OUTPUT=10.00

# --- Rate-limit counters (optional) ---
# Where @fastify/rate-limit keeps its counters. Unset means in process, which is
# correct for a single-process install and is the default.
#
# It matters the moment the API runs more than one replica: per-process counters
# mean each replica enforces the whole ceiling by itself, so three replicas serve
# three times the turns an account is entitled to — and nothing in any log says
# so, because each process is applying the limit exactly as written.
#
# `docker compose up -d redis` starts the local one on 6380 (6379 is left free
# for whatever else you already run).
# REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6380

# How many API containers `docker-compose.prod.yml` runs. Default 1.
#
# Raise it only on the API lane — AI_PROVIDER=anthropic-api. The subscription
# lane, which is what an unset AI_PROVIDER means, runs a `claude` subprocess
# against credentials in a shared volume, and two replicas would have two
# processes refreshing one OAuth token.
#
# Photos must be in a bucket before raising this (see S3_* below), or a photo
# taken on one replica is a 404 from the other.
# API_REPLICAS=2

# Accounts are email + password with server-side sessions. Set false once your
# own account exists to stop anyone else registering (the very first account is
# always allowed, or the server would be unusable).
ALLOW_SIGNUP=true

# Set true when serving over HTTPS so session cookies carry the Secure flag.
SECURE_COOKIES=false

# --- Signing in with Google (optional) ---
# Off unless both of these are set, in which case the sign-in screen grows a
# "Continue with Google" button. Create an OAuth client ID of type "Web
# application" at https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials and add the
# redirect URI below to it, byte for byte — Google matches it exactly.
#
# The scopes asked for are openid, email and profile. All three are
# non-sensitive, so the consent screen needs no review from Google.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=1234-abc.apps.googleusercontent.com
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=GOCSPX-...
#
# Where Google sends the browser back. Defaults to APP_URL + the path below,
# which is the web app rather than this API — and has to be: the callback sets
# the session cookie, and a cookie set on the API's own hostname is one the
# browser will never send to the app. Only set this if your deployment puts the
# web app somewhere APP_URL does not describe.
# GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=https://daysofar.com/api/auth/google/callback
#
# Extra URL prefixes the *native* app may be handed a finished sign-in at,
# comma separated. The app's own scheme (`daysofar://`) is always allowed and
# needs nothing here; this exists for `exp://`, which is the only address Expo
# Go can be reached at and therefore the only way to try Google sign-in on a
# phone without making a build.
#
# It is opt-in rather than a default because the value arrives in the query
# string of an anonymous GET: a start URL naming somebody else's redirect,
# walked through by a signed-in victim, would end with a sign-in code arriving
# where that somebody is listening. Leave it unset in production. http and
# https are refused whatever is put here.
# MOBILE_REDIRECT_PREFIXES=exp://

# Browser origins allowed to call the API cross-origin with credentials, comma
# separated. Only needed once the API answers on its own hostname: a browser
# sends the session cookie with these requests, so reflecting whatever Origin
# arrives would let any site on the internet act as a signed-in user. The mobile
# apps are deliberately absent — they send no Origin and CORS does not apply.
# WEB_ORIGINS=https://daysofar.com

# Which peers may be believed when they set X-Forwarded-For. Unset means none,
# which is right with nothing in front. Behind a reverse proxy set it, or the
# rate limiter reads the proxy's address on every request and buckets every
# anonymous caller in the world together. Takes a CIDR or one of proxy-addr's
# named ranges; `uniquelocal` is right for a proxy on a private Docker network.
# Avoid `true`: it trusts the whole forwarded chain, letting a caller name its
# own address and so choose its own rate-limit bucket.
# TRUST_PROXY=uniquelocal

# Who can open /admin — a comma-separated list of emails. Leave it unset and the
# first account created holds the panel, which is what you want on a personal
# install. Setting it replaces that fallback entirely rather than adding to it.
# ADMIN_EMAILS=you@example.com

# Where uploaded meal photos are written, when they go to local disk.
UPLOAD_DIR=./uploads

# --- Meal photos in a bucket (optional) ---
# Leave all four unset and photos stay in UPLOAD_DIR, which is right for one
# process with a volume. Set them and new photos go to the bucket instead —
# existing ones keep being read off disk, so this is a switch rather than a
# migration, and nothing needs backfilling.
#
# It matters once there is a second replica: a photo written to one container's
# disk is a permanent 404 from the other. Unlike a rate-limit counter, that miss
# cannot be shrugged off.
#
# Written S3-shaped because any S3-compatible store works. Cloudflare R2 is the
# one to reach for — egress is free, and egress is what bills you when a photo
# is looked at more than once. From the R2 dashboard: the endpoint is
# https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com, and the two keys come from
# "Manage API tokens" with Object Read & Write scoped to the one bucket. Keep
# the bucket private — do not enable the public r2.dev URL. The API signs a
# short-lived link per read, which is what authorises it.
#
# All four together or none: a half-configured bucket refuses to boot rather
# than failing at the first photo somebody takes.
# S3_ENDPOINT=https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
# S3_BUCKET=calorytracker-photos
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# R2 has no regions but SigV4 still signs the field, so it defaults to `auto`.
# A real S3 bucket names its region here.
# S3_REGION=auto

# --- Email (Resend) ---
# Optional. With no key the server writes each message to its log instead of
# sending it — including reset links, which is exactly what you want locally.
# Nothing in the product is gated on an email arriving, so leaving this unset is
# a supported way to run a personal install.
#
# Create a key at https://resend.com/api-keys
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_...
#
# The From header. The default is Resend's shared sandbox sender, which works
# with a fresh account and no DNS but will only deliver to the address that owns
# the account. Set your own once you have verified a domain.
# EMAIL_FROM=Day So Far <notifications@yourdomain.com>
#
# Where replies go, if not to the From address. Worth setting: several of these
# messages end with "reply to this email", and a no-reply sender makes that a lie.
# EMAIL_REPLY_TO=support@yourdomain.com
#
# --- Receiving ---
# Signs the inbound webhook Resend posts to /email/inbound. Copy it from the
# webhook you create in the Resend dashboard. Unset means the endpoint refuses
# everything, which is the right default: it is public and writable, and the
# signature is the only thing authenticating the caller.
#
# Receiving also needs an MX record on the domain — see the README. Without one
# the "reply to this email" line in several of these messages is a lie.
# RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...
#
# Sends every message to this address instead of the real recipient, with the
# intended one in the subject. Makes the sandbox sender's one-address limit
# usable for testing the whole flow end to end.
# EMAIL_REDIRECT_TO=you@example.com
#
# --- Sending rate ---
# How many requests a second the *scheduled* mail will make: the Monday review
# and the nudge. Interactive mail — a reset link, a confirmation code — is not
# governed by it and never queues behind a morning's worth of weekly reviews.
#
# The default of 2 is Resend's own limit on a new account. Raise this to match
# your plan once you have asked them for more, and note what it buys: at 2/s a
# thousand reviews take eight minutes to post, which is nothing beside the model
# time in front of them — at ten thousand it is eighty, which is not.
# EMAIL_MAX_RPS=2

# --- Billing ---
# RevenueCat sits between the app and the two stores, and its webhook is what
# turns a purchase into `users.plan`. Nothing here is needed to run the app: with
# it unset the entitlement seam still works, every plan is whatever the column
# says, and the paywall reports that this build cannot reach a store.
#
# The shared secret RevenueCat sends as the `Authorization` header on its
# webhook. It is a value YOU invent and paste into their dashboard next to the
# URL — it is not issued by them, and there is nowhere to look it up afterwards,
# so generate it once and keep it:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Unset means POST /billing/revenuecat refuses every delivery. That is the only
# safe default — the endpoint is public and the body names an account and a
# tier, so anybody who found the path could otherwise grant themselves Coach
# forever — but it is also silent: RevenueCat records a non-2xx and retries, and
# the buyer's plan simply never changes.
# REVENUECAT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
#
# Whether to honour purchases the store marks SANDBOX. Defaults on outside
# production, which is where somebody testing the flow wants it. Never set this
# true on a deployment that takes real money: a sandbox purchase costs nothing,
# so honouring one is a free Coach plan for anybody with a tester account.
# BILLING_ACCEPT_SANDBOX=true
#
# The phone's half is not here — it is `EXPO_PUBLIC_REVENUECAT_IOS_KEY` and
# `EXPO_PUBLIC_REVENUECAT_ANDROID_KEY`, which are public SDK keys baked into the
# app at build time and belong in `apps/mobile/.env` or in EAS. See
# `apps/mobile/lib/billing.ts`.

# --- Barcodes ---
# Open Food Facts needs neither a key nor an account and is what the scanner
# uses out of the box. Their policy asks callers to identify themselves as
# `AppName/Version (contact)` and throttles the generic agents that do not; the
# default is built from APP_URL below, which is usually enough.
# BARCODE_USER_AGENT=DaySoFar/1.0 (https://yourdomain.com)
#
# USDA FoodData Central, consulted only when Open Food Facts came back empty.
# Worth setting for American users and pointless without them: OFF covers the EU
# shelf well on its own, and this is what answers the branded US half. Free key,
# no card: https://fdc.nal.usda.gov/api-key-signup.html
# FDC_API_KEY=

# Where the browser reaches this deployment. Every link in an email is built
# from it, so it must be the address a person can actually open — not the API's
# own hostname behind the proxy.
APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# --- Web (apps/web/.env.local) ---
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:4000

# --- Opening links in the app instead of the browser ---
# Set these and `https://yourdomain.com/progress` — the button in the weekly
# review email — opens the app for anyone who has it installed, and the web app
# for everyone else. Leave them unset and every link keeps opening the browser,
# which is a working state, not a broken one: the two `.well-known` routes serve
# nothing at all until both are filled in, deliberately, because a *malformed*
# site association is cached by Apple for days where a missing one costs
# nothing. Neither value is a secret — both are published to anyone who asks.
#
# The Apple team, from developer.apple.com → Membership details. Ten characters.
# APPLE_TEAM_ID=
#
# The SHA-256 of the certificate the Android app is signed with, colon-separated
# — `eas credentials`, or Play Console → Setup → App integrity. Comma-separate
# more than one while migrating signing keys, or links break for whichever half
# of your users has the other build.
# ANDROID_CERT_SHA256=
#
# Which paths this covers is decided in `apps/web/lib/app-links.ts`, and it is
# deliberately a short allowlist: sign-in links, the OAuth callback and the
# unsubscribe page all have to stay in the browser.

# --- Deployment host only (docker-compose.prod.yml) ---
# None of these are read by the application. They are consumed by
# `docker-compose.prod.yml` itself, which is why they have no effect in a local
# checkout and why they are listed apart from everything above.

# The database password, and the only variable in this file the production
# stack refuses to start without — compose declares it `:?`, so a missing one
# fails the deploy immediately rather than booting something half-configured.
# Generate a long random string; nothing reads it back.
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# The role and database compose creates and the API connects as. Both default
# to the values below, so set them only if this host needs different ones —
# and note that changing either after the first `up` does not rename anything
# in the existing volume, it just stops matching what is in there.
# POSTGRES_USER=ct
# POSTGRES_DB=calorytracker

# Container-local time. Everything is stored in UTC; this is what the API's
# scheduler and the log timestamps read as local.
# TZ=Europe/Sofia

# Ceiling for the rate-limiter's Redis. Everything in it is a counter with a
# TTL of minutes, so the default is generous already; it evicts rather than
# refusing writes when full.
# REDIS_MAXMEMORY=128mb

# A note on what NOT to put in the host's .env: EMAIL_REDIRECT_TO. It is a
# development convenience, and on a deployment that can really send mail it
# quietly rewrites every recipient to one address — including the confirmation
# codes new accounts are waiting for.

# --- Posting to TikTok (optional, ops tooling only) ---
# Read by scripts/tiktok/*, never by the application. Unset means the scripts
# refuse to run and nothing else notices.
#
# The upload lands in the account's DRAFTS, not on the profile. Posting straight
# to the profile needs the `video.publish` scope, which TikTok grants only to
# clients that have passed its audit — so the value here is the file transfer off
# this machine, not automated posting. See scripts/tiktok/README.md §0.
#
# Both values come from an app at https://developers.tiktok.com, created while
# signed in AS @daysofarapp — whoever authorises is who the videos post as. The
# app needs the Content Posting API product *and* Login Kit: Content Posting
# alone has no way to issue a token.
# TIKTOK_CLIENT_KEY=
# TIKTOK_CLIENT_SECRET=
#
# Matched byte for byte against the URI registered in the developer portal, and
# TikTok will generally not register an http://localhost one. An https URL on a
# domain you own is the reliable choice, and NOTHING HAS TO SERVE IT — the
# browser lands there with ?code= in the query string, and a 404 page carries
# the code as well as a real one. auth.mjs asks you to paste that URL back.
#
# A loopback URI is also supported for the app types that accept one, in which
# case auth.mjs serves the port itself and no copying is needed.
# TIKTOK_REDIRECT_URI=https://daysofar.com/tiktok/callback
